From 8dec0b60a0f42b189c38bf051bfb730adae964bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:20:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix nested conditional in the middle operand of ?:

The middle operand of a conditional expression was parsed as a
logical-OR expression, so an unparenthesised nested ?: (or an
assignment or comma expression) between '?' and ':' failed with
"':' expected". Both C and C++ define that operand as a full
expression: a ? b ? x : y : z means a ? (b ? x : y) : z.

Parse the middle operand with ParseExpression and build EX_COMMA
nodes for any comma operators that follow. EX_COMMA rather than
EX_LIST, because a ?: with a constant condition folds to its middle
operand, and both the call argument walker and the global
optimizer's comma hoisting treat an EX_LIST in an argument position
as several arguments.

The tests cover ?: nested in the middle and last operands, a comma
and an assignment in the middle operand with side-effect checks,
a middle-operand comma (two and three operands) as the only, first
and last argument of a call with a run-time and with a constant
condition, constant expressions, that only the selected branch is
evaluated, and that the precedence of the first operand is
unchanged. The C++ test adds class and template cases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
